.TH KERO-HOST 1 "2026-09-24" "kero-host 0.1.0-alpha.1" "KERO User Manual"
.SH NAME
kero-host \- explicit repository knowledge environment command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kero-host
.RI [ --json ]
.RI [ --runtime " file" ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kero-host
operates on an explicitly selected KERO repository environment. It validates
the portable
.B kero.wasm
core before executing an operation. The runtime normally resides beside the
installed command; development may supply
.B --runtime
or
.B KERO_RUNTIME.
.PP
Repository targets and mount sources are always explicit. KERO does not scan
the machine, KERO home, or sibling directories for repositories or input.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init [path]
Initialize an eligible non-bare Git worktree. The command creates
.B .kero/config ,
.B .kero/data ,
and
.B .kero/mnt ,
but not runtime state.
.TP
.B repository status [path]
Classify one explicit path as not a repository, unsupported, unavailable,
enrolled, or eligible.
.TP
.B repository enroll [--policy ask|automatic|manual] [path]
Apply the selected enrollment policy to one explicit worktree.
.TP
.B mount inspect|create|add|add-home|list|remove|refresh|watch|sync|grant|conflict
Manage named local mounts. Mount inspect classifies one explicit path as an
in-format repository environment, marked global home, or out-of-format source.
Mount add materializes only an explicit repository source's local data. Mount
add-home materializes only an explicit marked global home's data. Both create
read-only mounted snapshots by default. `mount repair` revalidates an
unreleased JSON provenance record and replaces it with KST provenance while
preserving the prior complete snapshot on failure. `mount watch enable` opts one mount
into event-driven, debounced replacement snapshots; `mount refresh` is the
manual equivalent. `mount grant create|list|revoke` manages source-owned,
signed writable-sync grants. `mount sync` applies a grant-authorized pull or
push and blocks divergent edits. `mount conflict use-source|use-local|export`
performs an explicit resolution; export writes both complete trees outside the
mount. Watchers are not direct filesystem projections.
.TP
.B knowledge add|list|remove
Capture, inspect, or remove explicit local knowledge snapshots. Add accepts a
regular file or directory, rejects symbolic links and KERO-owned input, and
stores a deterministic content-addressed snapshot beneath
.B .kero/data/input .
.TP
.B setup [--identity reuse|configure-later]
Plan or apply global KERO-home creation. `--identity reuse` is the explicit
consent action that creates or reuses the local Ed25519 identity used by signed
writable-sync grants. Grant and sync commands never generate a new identity.
.TP
.B service start|status|stop
Control the per-user local KERO service. Ordinary commands repair a missing
service and send their request only through authenticated local IPC: a
current-user named pipe on Windows or owner-only Unix socket on Unix hosts.
The endpoint and token are disposable global-home runtime state; KERO does not
open a TCP or UDP listener or provide remote/cross-user access.
.PP
On Windows the installer creates a current-user Startup shortcut that starts
the host for every signed-in session. `service stop` does not leave a persistent KERO
installation stopped; it ensures the host is running.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit a machine-readable success or error envelope.
.TP
.B --runtime " file"
Use an explicit portable core artifact.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a completed operation. A nonzero status indicates invalid input,
an unavailable runtime, or a rejected repository/environment boundary.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cargo (1),
.BR git (1)
